# Hosts that are always blocked as a CONNECT target, regardless of
# filter_mode or any allow list (static or per-tenant dynamic). One
# hostname per line; a suffix match, same as allowlist_file/denylist_file.
#
# A permitted CONNECT tunnel isn't inspected — the proxy relays whatever
# bytes the client sends. So even a fully trusted allow-listed host could
# otherwise be used to run DNS-over-HTTPS or DNS-over-TLS through the
# tunnel, resolving arbitrary domains completely outside this proxy's own
# DNS filtering. This list exists to hold known public DoH/DoT resolver
# hostnames so an allow list can never accidentally (or deliberately)
# reopen that hole.
#
# This is a starting point, not exhaustive — review and extend it for
# your deployment. To allow one of these for a real, reviewed reason,
# remove the line; there is no separate override mechanism.

# Cloudflare
cloudflare-dns.com
one.one.one.one
mozilla.cloudflare-dns.com

# Google
dns.google

# Quad9
dns.quad9.net

# OpenDNS
doh.opendns.com

# NextDNS
dns.nextdns.io

# AdGuard
dns.adguard.com
unfiltered.adguard-dns.com

# CleanBrowsing
doh.cleanbrowsing.org
